// Lesson 2.1 — Coins: Triggers, Colliders & the StatManager
// Deep dive into OnTriggerEnter2D, setting up trigger colliders on pickups,
// comparing CompareTag vs tag ==, and building StatManager.cs from scratch.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>2.1 — Coins: Triggers, Colliders &amp; the StatManager</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Movement gets you around the world; now it is time to interact with it.
    Today you learn how Unity detects when two objects overlap, how to set up
    a coin with trigger colliders, and how to build a <code>StatManager</code> from
    scratch to keep score.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        In real life, two solid objects cannot occupy the same space. In a physics engine,
        detecting when shapes touch requires <strong>Colliders</strong>. But not all collisions
        are meant to stop you like a brick wall. Pickups like coins should let you walk right
        through them while recording an event.
    </p>

    <p>
        Unity provides a special mode on 2D colliders called <strong>Is Trigger</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Solid Collider (Is Trigger = unchecked):</strong> Bumps and blocks. Unity's physics engine stops the objects from penetrating.</li>
        <li><strong>Trigger Collider (Is Trigger = checked):</strong> Intangible zone. Objects pass freely through each other, and Unity fires the <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> message.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>When two objects overlap and at least one is a trigger, Unity executes this method:</p>
    <pre><code>private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code></pre>

    <p>
        Notice the parameter: <code>Collider2D other</code>. When the player runs into a coin,
        who is <code>other</code>? <code>other</code> is the collider belonging to the <em>coin</em>!
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Reading Unity Functions for Identification</h3>
        <p>
            When <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> fires, it fires for <em>anything</em> the player walks into —
            grass, doorways, hazards, or coins. How do we know what we just touched?
            Read these two ways to check in Unity:
        </p>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Example</th><th>How It Works</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CompareTag</code></td><td><code>other.CompareTag("Coin")</code></td><td>Optimized engine check. Validates the tag name and produces zero garbage collection memory.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>tag ==</code></td><td><code>other.tag == "Coin"</code></td><td>String comparison. Allocates temporary memory on every call and fails silently on typos.</td></tr>
        </table>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does the Unity documentation strongly recommend <code>other.CompareTag("Coin")</code> over <code>other.tag == "Coin"</code>?</li>
            <li>If you misspell a tag as <code>"coin"</code> (lowercase) with <code>CompareTag</code>, Unity throws an error alerting you that the tag does not exist. Why is an explicit error message much better for debugging than silent failure?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Notice how each function operates in isolation across completely different game systems.
        You do not copy a whole script; you learn what each function takes in, what it does, and how to combine them:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — Receiving Overlap Events on an Automatic Grocery Store Door:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Unity calls this method automatically whenever an object enters an 'Is Trigger' zone
private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D entrant)
{
    Debug.Log("Motion sensor triggered! Opening automatic doors for: " + entrant.name);
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — Validating Object Identity with Tags on a Vault Scanner:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Efficiently verifies whether an incoming collider carries a specific tag label
if (incomingItem.CompareTag("SecurityKeycard"))
{
    Debug.Log("Access granted to high-security vault.");
}</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Memory Cleanup with Destroy on a Spent Rocket Stage:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Removes the specified GameObject entirely from the scene and frees its memory
Destroy(spentBoosterStage.gameObject);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — Global Persistence with the Singleton Pattern on a Jukebox Manager:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// public static makes 'jukebox' globally accessible from any script in the game
public static JukeboxManager jukebox;

private void Awake()
{
    if (jukebox == null)
    {
        jukebox = this;
        DontDestroyOnLoad(gameObject); // Survives scene changes so music never stops
    }
    else
    {
        Destroy(gameObject); // Destroy duplicates if returning to the menu
    }
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In the automatic door snippet, what is the type of <code>entrant</code>? Why is it a <code>Collider2D</code> rather than a <code>GameObject</code>?</li>
            <li>In the rocket booster snippet, what happens if you write <code>Destroy(spentBoosterStage)</code> without <code>.gameObject</code>? (Hint: Does that destroy the whole rocket or just the single collider component?)</li>
            <li>In the jukebox snippet, how could any script in the entire game tell the jukebox to play a song without needing <code>GetComponent</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — Set up the Coin Prefab in the Inspector.</strong> Select <code>Assets/Prefabs/Coin</code>:</p>
    <ol>
        <li>Verify it has a 2D collider (e.g. <code>CircleCollider2D</code>).</li>
        <li>Check the box for <strong>Is Trigger</strong>. (A pickup must be an overlap zone, not a solid wall!).</li>
        <li>At the very top of the Inspector, verify its <strong>Tag</strong> is set to <strong>Coin</strong>. (If "Coin" is not listed, click <em>Add Tag...</em> and create it).</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>StatManager.cs</code>:</strong>
        <p><strong>Singleton Architecture &amp; State:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Where in <code>Assets/Scripts/</code> should you create this script, and what must the class name match?</li>
            <li>How do you make this manager globally accessible to any script without needing <code>GetComponent</code>? What static variable do you need to declare?</li>
            <li>What variable tracks the total coins collected? How do you make it visible in the Inspector for debugging while keeping it private?</li>
            <li>Which early lifecycle hook runs before <code>Start()</code> to establish singletons?</li>
            <li>What check ensures only one instance of <code>StatManager</code> exists across scenes? What function prevents this GameObject from being destroyed when loading new scenes? What should happen to duplicate managers?</li>
            <li>What public method should other scripts call to modify the coin count? How does it adjust the coin total and log the result?</li>
            <li>Where in the Unity scene hierarchy should this component be attached so it exists when the game begins?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>PlayerController.cs</code>:</strong>
        <p><strong>Handle Coin Trigger:</strong></p>
        <ul>
            <li>What function does this belong in?</li>
            <li>Should a coin pickup be a trigger or a solid collider?</li>
            <li>How do you check if the object collided with is a Coin?</li>
            <li>Before modifying stats, what safety check prevents errors if the manager is missing?</li>
            <li>What function on <code>StatManager</code> adds to the coin total?</li>
            <li>How do you destroy the collected coin? Is the coin <code>other.gameObject</code> or is it <code>gameObject</code>? (Hint: this script is attached to <code>gameObject</code>; we collided with <code>other</code>).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save both files and press Play in Unity. Walk your character into a coin!
        The coin should vanish upon contact, and the Unity Console will print the new coin total!
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Tracing Pickup Bugs</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if you uncheck <strong>Is Trigger</strong> on the Coin prefab? When the player walks into the coin, does <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> run?</li>
            <li>What happens if you accidentally write <code>Destroy(gameObject)</code> instead of <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code>? Which object vanishes from the game?</li>
            <li>What happens if you comment out <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code>? Why is a coin that gives money continuously every time you touch it a bug?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Destroy vs. SetActive</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In Unity documentation, look up <code>other.gameObject.SetActive(false)</code>. In <code>PlayerController.cs</code>, replace <code>Destroy(other.gameObject)</code> with <code>other.gameObject.SetActive(false)</code>. What happens when you collect a coin?</li>
            <li>In large professional games with thousands of coins or bullets, why is disabling objects (object pooling) often preferred over destroying and creating them repeatedly?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the difference between a solid collider and a trigger collider</li>
            <li>Explain what <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> does and identify what the <code>other</code> parameter refers to</li>
            <li>Explain why <code>other.CompareTag("Coin")</code> is preferred over <code>other.tag == "Coin"</code></li>
            <li>Create a new C# script (<code>StatManager.cs</code>) and attach it to a scene GameObject</li>
            <li>Commit your working coin pickup system to GitHub</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
